<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Griffith Joyner returns two weeks after her first visit. The sleep-deprived EEG two days after that visit showed intermittent left frontal sharp waves and slowing. MRI of the brain six days after the visit, with susceptibility-weighted imaging, shows a single 1.4 cm cavernous malformation in the left orbitofrontal cortex with a hemosiderin rim and an adjacent developmental venous anomaly, and no other lesion. Dr. Mehta-Sorensen of the epilepsy service saw her this morning. She has had no further seizure, spell or headache. She has not driven, showers rather than bathes, and has not swum. The records of her earlier seizure care have been requested and have not arrived. She is not pregnant (negative test at the first visit) and is not planning a pregnancy now. She has had no thoughts of self-harm. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EEG</td><td>Left frontal sharp waves and focal slowing</td></tr><tr><td>MRI</td><td>Single 1.4 cm left orbitofrontal cavernous malformation with a hemosiderin rim; no other lesion on susceptibility-weighted imaging</td></tr><tr><td>Since the last visit</td><td>No seizure; not driving</td></tr><tr><td>Coordination</td><td>Epilepsy consultation today; neurosurgical opinion requested</td></tr><tr><td>Her questions</td><td>Whether this is epilepsy, whether the lesion can be removed, and when she can train and drive</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

